Arguments: {"idea": {
  "Name": "language_detour_overthinking",
  "Title": "The Language Tax: Overthinking as a Pivot-Detour Overhead in Non-English Reasoning",
  "Short Hypothesis": "For the identical problem, reasoning models spend substantially more tokens when the question is posed in a low-resource / non-Latin-script language than in English, and this excess is not extra genuine reasoning but a 'pivot detour' overhead: tokens spent routing the input into an English-centered latent reasoning space (re-translating, restating, language-mixing) after the effective answer computation is already possible. This overhead is mechanistically driven by a language-specific representational component and can be causally reduced\u2014cutting tokens with no accuracy loss\u2014by ablating that component mid-generation.",
  "Related Work": "Multilingual reasoning work documents an accuracy gap favoring high-resource languages, attributes it to understanding failures, and mitigates it via translation/alignment for accuracy (Why Do Multilingual Reasoning Gaps Emerge 2025; SLAM 2025; Question Translation 2024; SoT 2025). Language-mixing work shows reasoning traces drift toward English/Han pivots, more so on harder/low-resource inputs, reflecting an English-centered latent pathway (Language Mixing in RLMs 2025; Large Reasoning Models Are (Not Yet) Multilingual Latent Reasoners 2026). Disentanglement work ablates language-specific hidden components to IMPROVE ACCURACY (When Less Language is More 2025). Efficiency/overthinking work (Stop Overthinking survey 2025; When More is Less 2025; TRACE 2025) is entirely English-only and studies verbosity/verification, not language. Crucially, no prior work (a) measures an EFFICIENCY (token/compute) gap across languages on matched problems, (b) decomposes non-English trace length into genuine-reasoning vs pivot-detour overhead, or (c) uses language-component ablation to REDUCE TOKENS at fixed accuracy. Our contribution unites the efficiency and multilingual literatures: we name and quantify language-dependent overthinking, attribute its excess specifically to a pivot detour, and turn the disentanglement direction (previously an accuracy tool) into an efficiency intervention.",
  "Abstract": "Reasoning language models 'overthink,' but studies of this waste are almost entirely English-only, while multilingual studies focus on accuracy gaps, not compute. We ask a simple, consequential question: for the exact same problem, does a model reason longer\u2014and waste more\u2014when the question is asked in a low-resource or non-Latin-script language than in English? We hypothesize a language tax: non-English prompts incur a pivot-detour overhead in which the model spends tokens routing the input into an English-centered latent reasoning space (re-translation, restatement, language-mixing) rather than doing extra genuine reasoning. Using matched multilingual math and science benchmarks (MGSM and translated MATH500/GPQA subsets across high- and low-resource, Latin and non-Latin scripts) on open reasoning models (DeepSeek-R1 distills 1.5B-14B, Qwen3), we first measure per-language token counts on identical problems, controlling for tokenizer effects via character/word-normalized budgets. We then decompose each non-English trace into genuine-reasoning tokens versus pivot-detour tokens using truncate-and-resume (when does the correct answer become recoverable?) and language-of-token classification, and test whether the cross-language token gap is concentrated in the detour, not in reasoning depth. Mechanistically, we locate a language-specific representational component via probing and difference-of-means, and causally test whether ablating it mid-generation collapses the detour, reducing tokens at fixed accuracy\u2014turning an accuracy-oriented disentanglement into an efficiency intervention. We compare against forced-English-reasoning and translate-first baselines on tokens-at-fixed-accuracy and latency. Our work reveals a hidden, equity-relevant compute cost paid by non-English users and offers an interpretable fix.",
  "Experiments": "1) Efficiency gap (behavior): Take matched problem sets\u2014MGSM (parallel across 11 languages) and machine-plus-human-verified translations of MATH500 and GPQA subsets\u2014covering high-resource Latin (English, French, German), high-resource non-Latin (Chinese), and low-resource non-Latin (Bengali, Swahili, Telugu) languages. For each identical problem generate reasoning traces per language. Metrics: mean tokens, and tokenizer-normalized effort (tokens per source-character and per reasoning-content-unit) to rule out that longer traces are just tokenizer fertility. Report per-language token gap vs English at MATCHED accuracy (subsample to equal-accuracy strata). Confirm the gap is not merely accuracy-driven. 2) Detour decomposition: (a) Truncate-and-resume: at each step, force termination and answer; record the 'effective solve step' (first step after which the answer is recoverable). Define genuine-reasoning length = tokens up to effective solve step, and overhead = tokens after. (b) Language-of-token classification: tag each token's script/language (fastText/script heuristics) and count 're-translation/restatement' spans and language-mixing switches. Hypothesis: the cross-language token gap is dominated by overhead/detour tokens, and effective-solve-step (in normalized units) is similar across languages, i.e., genuine reasoning depth is language-invariant while the detour is language-dependent. Correlate detour size with language resource level and script. 3) Pivot-detour signature: Using logit-lens on intermediate states, test whether non-English traces show an early switch of internal predicted-answer language to English/Han before verbal answer emerges (replicating/extending the English-centered latent pathway), and whether the detour tokens coincide with this internal switch. 4) Location: Train a linear probe / difference-of-means to isolate a language-specific direction in middle-layer residual stream (high-resource vs low-resource prompts, English vs non-English), reproducing the disentanglement setup. Validate that projecting it out preserves a latent-answer probe (reasoning intact) while degrading surface-language identification. 5) Causal intervention (efficiency): During generation on non-English prompts, ablate/project out the language-specific component at middle layers after the input-understanding phase. Measure whether the pivot detour collapses: metrics = tokens, detour-token fraction, language-mixing count, and accuracy. Compare against baselines: no intervention, forced-English-reasoning via constrained decoding (Language Mixing 2025), translate-first (Selective Translation 2025), and a matched random-direction ablation. Key metric: tokens-at-fixed-accuracy and wall-clock latency reduction per language, with accuracy retention. 6) Specificity/failure: verify ablation does not hurt genuinely-hard low-resource cases where understanding is the bottleneck (measure accuracy on hardest strata; check for underthinking). 7) Generalization: transfer the language component and intervention across datasets, scripts, and model scales without retraining; test whether one shared low-resource-language direction generalizes across several low-resource languages.",
  "Risk Factors and Limitations": "Translated benchmarks may introduce artifacts or altered difficulty; we mitigate with human-verified translations for a subset and report results on the parallel-by-construction MGSM separately. Tokenizer fertility confounds raw token counts; we control via character/word-normalized effort and matched-accuracy comparisons, but residual confounding is possible. The genuine-vs-detour split relies on truncate-and-resume, which is noisy and compute-heavy; we subsample steps and use two overhead definitions (resume-based and language-tag-based) for robustness. The pivot-detour hypothesis may be wrong\u2014extra tokens could reflect genuinely harder understanding rather than a routing overhead, which would be an informative negative result and still a novel efficiency-gap characterization. Language-specific ablation could damage understanding on low-resource inputs (underthinking), especially where the detour is functionally necessary; the specificity and hardest-strata analyses guard against this and may reveal the intervention only helps mid-difficulty items. Effects may be model-family and script specific, and low-resource-language coverage in open models is limited."
}}
