mpute cost paid by non-English users and offers an interpretable fix.", 'Experiments': "1) Efficiency gap (behavior): Take matched problem sets—MGSM (parallel across 11 languages) and machine-plus-human-verified translations of MATH500 and GPQA subsets—covering high-resource Latin (English, French, German), high-resource non-Latin (Chinese), and low-resource non-Latin (Bengali, Swahili, Telugu) languages. For each identical problem generate reasoning traces per language. Metrics: mean tokens, and tokenizer-normalized effort (tokens per source-character and per reasoning-content-unit) to rule out that longer traces are just tokenizer fertility. Report per-language token gap vs English at MATCHED accuracy (subsample to equal-accuracy strata). Confirm the gap is not merely accuracy-driven. 2) Detour decomposition: (a) Truncate-and-resume: at each step, force termination and answer; record the 'effective solve step' (first step after which the answer is recoverable). Define genuine-reasoning length = tokens up to effective solve step, and overhead = tokens after. (b) Language-of-token classification: tag each token's script/language (fastText/script heuristics) and count 're-translation/restatement' spans and language-mixing switches. Hypothesis: the cross-language token gap is dominated by overhead/detour tokens, and effective-solve-step (in normalized units) is similar across languages, i.e., genuine reasoning depth is language-invariant while the detour is language-dependent. Correlate detour size with language resource level and script. 3) Pivot-detour signature: Using logit-lens on intermediate states, test whether non-English traces show an early switch of internal predicted-answer language to English/Han before verbal answer emerges (replicating/extending the English-centered latent pathway), and whether the detour tokens coincide with this internal switch. 4) Location: Train a linear probe / difference-of-means to isolate a language-specific direction in middle-