king artifact and offers an interpretable, training-free fix.", 'Experiments': "1) Establish the fragmentation-length effect (behavioral): For each seed math problem (GSM8K, MATH500, arithmetic-heavy word problems), generate content-identical variants that change ONLY how numbers are tokenized: (a) native BPE, (b) digit-spaced ('1 2 3 4'), (c) comma/underscore-grouped ('1,234'), (d) single-digit forced segmentation. Verify with the model's own tokenizer that fragment counts differ (record fragments-per-number) while decoded text and correct answer are semantically identical. Generate reasoning traces per variant. Metrics: reasoning-token count, accuracy, per variant. Hypothesis: higher fragmentation -> more reasoning tokens at equal accuracy. Report effect size within-problem.\n2) Dissociation from difficulty: Fit a mixed-effects model of reasoning length with random effect for problem identity (intrinsic difficulty) and fixed effect for fragment count. Test for a significant fragmentation effect after accounting for problem identity; report variance attributable to fragmentation vs difficulty. Control for raw prompt token-length (digit-spacing adds tokens) by including it as a covariate and by comparing formats matched on prompt length.\n3) Rule out genuine-reasoning explanation: Use truncate-and-resume to estimate minimal-solve length per variant; test whether fragmentation increases the WASTED tokens (beyond minimal solve) rather than the genuine-reasoning portion. Also check whether fragmentation-induced extra tokens are disproportionately narration/re-reading of the numbers (language-tag / logit-lens analysis).\n4) Location - fragmentation-sensitive length probe: At the prompt boundary (last prompt token, before any reasoning token), extract residual-stream activations and train a linear probe to predict reasoning length. Test whether, for identical problems, the probe's prediction rises with fragment count. Compare to a true-difficulty probe (trained on pass-rate). Test separability via principal angles between the fragmentation-length direction and the difficulty direction; report predictive gain in a joint model. Layer sweep to localize.\n5) Causal intervention - patching: Patch the prompt-boundary residual state from a low-fragmentation variant into the matched high-fragmentation context (same problem) and measure whether reasoning length drops toward the low-fragmentation level. Controls: matched-random-direction patch; off-target accuracy preserved. Report sign, dose, specificity.\n6) Causal intervention - debiasing steering (training-free fix): Derive a fragmentation-length steering direction (difference-of-means between low- and high-fragmentation boundary states) and steer high-fragmentation prompts to cancel the inflation. Compare tokens-at-equal-accuracy against no intervention, simple re-encoding of numbers into fewer tokens (a deployable preprocessing fix), and fixed truncation. Metrics: accuracy, mean tokens, tokens-at-equal-accuracy, wall-clock speedup.\n7) Practical payoff and generalization: Quantify, over a mixed problem stream, the tokens wasted purely due to native tokenizer fragmentation vs an oracle low-fragmentation encoding. Test whether a simple input-side re-encoding (digit grouping into single tokens) is a free efficiency win. Transfer probe/steering across datasets and model scales without retraining; test whether the effect is stronger for models with coarser numeral tokenization.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': "The fragmentation effect could be an artifact of prompt token-length (digit-spacing lengthens the prompt) rather than segmentation per se; the prompt-length covariate, length-matched format comparisons, and the single-digit-vs-grouped contrast (which vary fragmentation at similar or opposite prompt lengths) are designed to isolate this, and a null after controls is an informative negative result. Number re-formatting could inadvertently change accuracy or difficulty; we verify answer/accuracy invariance and use tokenizer-verified content-identical variants. The fragmentation-length signal may be inseparable from a true-difficulty signal if the model genuinely finds fragmented numbers harder to parse (which would still be a novel, actionable phenomenon but weaken the 'artifact' framing); the separability analysis and patching address this. Steering/patching can push activations off-distribution and confound the accuracy read; matched-random-direction controls and sample-validity checks are included. Effects likely depend on the model's numeral-tokenization scheme and may be small for models already using clean single-digit tokenization (itself an informative boundary condition). Reasoning-length measurement requires clear think/answer delimiters, limiting to models with explicit thinking segments; truncate-and-resume is compute-heavy, so we subsample."}

