Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "subgoal_schedule_myopia", "Title": "Solving the Wrong Subgoal First: Overthinking as a Dependency-Scheduling Failure in Reasoning Models", "Short Hypothesis": "A problem's subgoals form a dependency DAG whose prerequisites impose a partial order; reasoning models schedule subgoals myopically rather than dependency-respectingly, frequently attacking a subgoal before its prerequisites are established. This premature scheduling wastes tokens on flailing and re-derivation. The model already carries an internal, decodable 'prerequisite-readiness' signal at each subgoal-onset that it fails to consult, and steering generation toward dependency-respecting order cuts tokens without hurting accuracy.", "Related Work": "Probing work shows LLM hidden states encode reasoning-DAG geometry (From Chains to DAGs, 2026) and that models are largely myopic/incremental planners with weak global planning (No Global Plan in CoT, 2026; Internal Planning: Horizon and Branch Awareness, 2025; Detecting and Characterizing Planning, 2025). Efficiency work characterizes overthinking as an inverted-U length effect (When More is Less, 2025) or as decorative/redundant steps (Thought Anchors; TTS). A shortest-path study shows next-token predictors are biased toward systematically inefficient (backtracking) traces (2025). Crucially, none connect the problem's *dependency structure* to *compute waste*: no prior work identifies suboptimal subgoal *ordering* (attacking a subgoal before its prerequisites) as a distinct, quantifiable source of overthinking, nor tests whether a decodable prerequisite-readiness signal governs it, nor intervenes on scheduling to save compute. This differs from my prior proposals (which target stopping, verification, length priors, and framing effects) by targeting the *order* in which reasoning is performed. It also goes beyond DAG-probing (which is diagnostic and static) by tying the encoded DAG to a behavioral scheduling failure and a causal efficiency intervention.", "Abstract": "Reasoning models overthink, but the role of *ordering* in that waste is unexamined. Many problems decompose into subgoals with prerequisite dependencies\u2014a directed acyclic graph whose topological orders are the valid schedules. We hypothesize that reasoning models schedule subgoals myopically: they frequently begin a subgoal before its prerequisites are established, then flail or re-derive, spending tokens that a dependency-respecting order would avoid. We further hypothesize the model already encodes, at each subgoal onset, a prerequisite-readiness signal (are the inputs this subgoal needs already available in context?) that it does not act on. Using multi-step problems with recoverable dependency structure (GSM8K, MATH500, and templated multi-constraint word/logic problems where we can annotate a ground-truth subgoal DAG) on open reasoning models (DeepSeek-R1 distills 1.5B-14B, Qwen3), we (1) segment traces into subgoals, align them to DAG nodes, and measure a scheduling-inversion rate (subgoals started before their prerequisites) and its token cost via truncate-and-resume and re-derivation detection; (2) show inversions predict wasted tokens beyond length or decorative-step baselines; (3) train a linear probe at subgoal onset to decode prerequisite-readiness and test its separability from a generic difficulty/confidence probe; (4) causally validate via activation patching\u2014patching a high-readiness state into a premature-onset context should shift the model to a ready subgoal; and (5) deploy a training-free scheduling steer that, when readiness is low, nudges the model to defer the premature subgoal, cutting tokens at equal accuracy versus length-aware and reactive-pruning baselines. Our work reframes a slice of overthinking as a dependency-scheduling failure with a decodable, steerable internal cause.", "Experiments": "1) Subgoal DAG annotation: Select problems with recoverable dependency structure\u2014templated multi-constraint word problems and small logic/scheduling puzzles (where the subgoal DAG is known by construction) plus GSM8K/MATH500 (where we annotate subgoal dependencies via solution parsing + LLM-judge, validated by human spot-checks). Each subgoal node has prerequisite nodes (quantities/facts it consumes). 2) Trace segmentation and alignment: Generate reasoning traces; segment into subgoals by step delimiters; align each generated subgoal to a DAG node (semantic matching + LLM-judge). Define a scheduling-inversion = a subgoal begun before all its prerequisite nodes have been established earlier in the trace. Metrics: per-trace inversion rate across datasets/model sizes. 3) Cost of inversions: For each inversion, measure downstream waste: (a) truncate-and-resume to estimate minimal-solve length under a dependency-respecting order (by forcing prerequisite subgoals first via prompt), (b) recompute/re-derivation detection (value-producing statements re-derived after a premature attempt), (c) dead-end/backtrack detection. Regress wasted tokens on inversion rate vs baselines: trace length, decorative-step fraction (TTS-style), and a difficulty probe; compare R^2. 4) Prerequisite-readiness probing (Location): At each subgoal-onset token boundary, extract residual-stream activations before the subgoal is generated; train a linear probe to predict whether the upcoming subgoal's prerequisites are already satisfied in context (label from DAG alignment). Metrics: AUROC/accuracy; layer sweep. Test separability from (i) a generic difficulty probe (pass-rate) and (ii) a confidence probe, via principal angles and joint-model predictive gain. Key claim: readiness is decodable yet the model still commits inversions\u2014evidence of an unconsulted signal. 5) Causal validation (Causal Intervention): Use activation patching to identify components carrying readiness; patch a high-readiness state (from a context where a ready subgoal is available) into a premature-onset context and test whether the model switches to a dependency-ready subgoal (inversion avoided, tokens dropped). Controls: matched-random-direction patch; off-target accuracy preserved; report sign, dose, specificity. 6) Scheduling steer (training-free intervention): Online, at each subgoal onset read the readiness probe; if low (predicted inversion), apply a steer/nudge (steer along the readiness direction, or inject a terse 'first establish prerequisites' cue) to defer the premature subgoal. Evaluate on all datasets. Baselines: no intervention, length-aware filtering (When More is Less), reactive pruning (Think Clearly), decorative-step skipping (TTS), fixed truncation. Metrics: accuracy (overall and hardest strata), mean tokens, inversion-rate reduction, token-reduction-at-equal-accuracy, wall-clock speedup. 7) Failure analysis: cases where an apparent inversion is actually beneficial exploration (the model probes a subgoal to discover what prerequisites it needs); measure accuracy impact of suppressing these and tune the steer to preserve genuine exploration. 8) Generalization: transfer probe/steer across datasets and model scales without retraining; test whether inversion rate correlates with the DAG's width/depth (harder-to-schedule DAGs \u2192 more inversions).", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "Annotating a ground-truth subgoal DAG for open math is noisy; we anchor the strongest claims on templated problems and puzzles with known structure, using GSM8K/MATH500 as corroboration with human-validated annotations. Aligning generated subgoals to DAG nodes is imperfect; we use semantic matching + LLM-judge with agreement reporting. Some scheduling 'inversions' may be legitimate exploration (probing a subgoal to learn its prerequisites), so suppressing them could hurt accuracy; the failure analysis and steer thresholds explicitly guard this, and finding that inversions are largely beneficial would be an informative negative result. The readiness signal may be inseparable from generic difficulty/confidence, weakening the mechanistic framing; the separability analysis and causal patching address this. Steering could push activations off-distribution and introduce errors; matched-random-direction controls and sample-validity checks are included. Intervention gains may not exceed strong length-aware or pruning baselines, but the scheduling-failure diagnosis (a named, quantifiable, dependency-structured source of overthinking) is a standalone contribution. Results may be model-family and scale specific, and reasoning-length measurement requires clear think/answer delimiters; truncate-and-resume is compute-heavy, so we subsample."}}
