l switches to a dependency-ready subgoal (inversion avoided, tokens dropped). Controls: matched-random-direction patch; off-target accuracy preserved; report sign, dose, specificity. 6) Scheduling steer (training-free intervention): Online, at each subgoal onset read the readiness probe; if low (predicted inversion), apply a steer/nudge (steer along the readiness direction, or inject a terse 'first establish prerequisites' cue) to defer the premature subgoal. Evaluate on all datasets. Baselines: no intervention, length-aware filtering (When More is Less), reactive pruning (Think Clearly), decorative-step skipping (TTS), fixed truncation. Metrics: accuracy (overall and hardest strata), mean tokens, inversion-rate reduction, token-reduction-at-equal-accuracy, wall-clock speedup. 7) Failure analysis: cases where an apparent inversion is actually beneficial exploration (the model probes a subgoal to discover what prerequisites it needs); measure accuracy impact of suppressing these and tune the steer to preserve genuine exploration. 8) Generalization: transfer probe/steer across datasets and model scales without retraining; test whether inversion rate correlates with the DAG's width/depth (harder-to-schedule DAGs → more inversions).", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': "Annotating a ground-truth subgoal DAG for open math is noisy; we anchor the strongest claims on templated problems and puzzles with known structure, using GSM8K/MATH500 as corroboration with human-validated annotations. Aligning generated subgoals to DAG nodes is imperfect; we use semantic matching + LLM-judge with agreement reporting. Some scheduling 'inversions' may be legitimate exploration (probing a subgoal to learn its prerequisites), so suppressing them could hurt accuracy; the failure analysis and steer thresholds explicitly guard this, and finding that inversions are largely beneficial would be an informative negative result. The readiness signal may be inseparable from generic difficulty/confidenc