Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "conciseness_capability_entanglement", "Title": "The Conciseness Cliff: Why Global Brevity Steering Fails Because the Concise Direction Is Entangled with Difficulty-Appropriate Elaboration", "Short Hypothesis": "Training-free brevity steering has a sharp accuracy cliff not because the steering strength is mis-tuned, but because the 'concise-reasoning' direction is geometrically entangled with a 'difficulty-appropriate elaboration' capability in the same activation subspace: pushing toward brevity necessarily suppresses the model's own signal for when a problem needs more thinking. Disentangling brevity from this elaboration component yields steering that compresses easy reasoning while preserving hard-problem elaboration, eliminating the cliff.", "Related Work": "A wave of training-free brevity-steering methods treat conciseness as a single (often linear) direction to inject: ASC (steer residual stream, KL-bounded strength), Efficiency Steering (single contrastive direction), FlowSteer (nonlinear flow-matching transport), STU-PID (PID-controlled adaptive strength), and GLoRE (long-CoT is a general, transferable capability across domains). All of these either (a) assume brevity is a clean, universally-good direction and manage only its magnitude, or (b) add an external controller/classifier to modulate strength reactively. None asks WHY fixed steering has an accuracy cliff at the mechanistic level, nor whether the brevity direction is intrinsically coupled to the model's internal 'how much to elaborate' signal. GLoRE shows the long-CoT capability is a general encoded direction; we hypothesize the concise direction partially anti-aligns with a difficulty-appropriate elaboration capability, so global steering is not merely too strong but structurally destructive on hard problems. Our contribution is a mechanistic account of the failure mode (entanglement), a geometric measurement of it (principal angles / overlap between the brevity direction and an elaboration-capability direction), and a disentangled steering intervention that removes the harmful component — distinct from all prior 'steer harder / steer adaptively' methods.", "Abstract": "Training-free brevity steering promises to cut reasoning tokens with no retraining, but every method shares a fragile behavior: past a threshold, accuracy collapses sharply, especially on hard problems. Prior work manages this only by tuning or adaptively controlling the steering magnitude. We propose a mechanistic explanation: the 'concise-reasoning' steering direction is geometrically entangled with a difficulty-appropriate elaboration capability the model uses to decide how much to think, so pushing toward brevity does not merely shorten output — it suppresses the internal signal that hard problems need more reasoning. This predicts an asymmetric cliff (harmless on easy problems, destructive on hard ones) and explains why global steering is brittle. Across GSM8K, MATH500, AIME, and GPQA on open reasoning models (DeepSeek-R1 distills 1.5B-14B, Qwen3), we (1) reproduce the brevity direction (ASC/Efficiency-Steering style) and map the accuracy-vs-strength cliff stratified by difficulty; (2) extract an elaboration-capability direction (contrasting hard-solved-with-long-reasoning vs easy-solved-short traces) and measure geometric overlap/anti-alignment with the brevity direction via principal angles; (3) causally test entanglement by projecting the brevity direction into the elaboration-orthogonal subspace and steering; and (4) show disentangled steering compresses easy reasoning while preserving hard-problem elaboration, flattening the cliff versus ASC, Efficiency Steering, and STU-PID. Our work reframes the brevity-accuracy tradeoff as a representational entanglement problem and offers an interpretable, training-free fix.", "Experiments": "1) Reproduce brevity direction and cliff: Extract a concise-reasoning steering direction via difference-of-means over paired verbose/concise traces (ASC/Efficiency-Steering protocol) on a calibration set. Sweep steering strength and measure accuracy and mean tokens, STRATIFIED by problem difficulty (GSM8K/MATH500 easy strata vs AIME/GPQA hard strata). Metric: locate the accuracy cliff per difficulty stratum; test the prediction that the cliff onset (in strength) is much earlier for hard problems than easy ones (asymmetric cliff). 2) Extract elaboration-capability direction: Build contrastive pairs isolating difficulty-appropriate elaboration — hard problems SOLVED CORRECTLY with long reasoning vs easy problems solved correctly with short reasoning — and compute a difference-of-means 'elaboration' direction per layer. Validate it: steering ALONG it should lengthen reasoning preferentially on problems the model treats as hard, and a probe along it should predict per-problem reasoning length beyond a raw difficulty probe. 3) Geometric entanglement measurement (Location): Compute principal angles / cosine overlap between the brevity direction and the elaboration direction across layers; test the hypothesis that they are significantly anti-aligned (brevity partially = negative elaboration). Compare to matched-random-direction pairs as a null. Also decompose the brevity direction into its projection onto the elaboration direction (harmful component) and its orthogonal complement (benign compression component). 4) Causal test of entanglement (Causal Intervention): (a) Steer along the FULL brevity direction; (b) steer along the elaboration-ORTHOGONAL component only; (c) steer along the elaboration-PARALLEL component only. Prediction: (c) alone reproduces most of the hard-problem accuracy damage with little compression benefit, while (b) gives compression with a much later/flatter cliff — demonstrating the harmful entangled component causes the cliff. Report sign, dose-response, and specificity (matched-random projection controls). 5) Disentangled steering intervention: Deploy training-free steering with the elaboration-orthogonal brevity component (optionally difficulty-gated using the elaboration probe). Evaluate on GSM8K, MATH500, AIME, GPQA. Baselines: no steering, full-direction ASC, Efficiency Steering, STU-PID, FlowSteer where reproducible, fixed truncation. Metrics: accuracy (overall and per difficulty stratum), mean tokens, token-reduction-at-equal-accuracy, and cliff position (steering strength at which hard-stratum accuracy drops 5%). Key claim: disentangled steering Pareto-dominates full-direction steering by flattening the hard-problem cliff. 6) Generalization: transfer both directions and the disentangled intervention across datasets and model scales without recomputation; test whether the entanglement geometry is stable across model families.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "The brevity and elaboration directions may turn out to be largely orthogonal (no entanglement), refuting the mechanistic claim — this would be an informative negative result showing the cliff is purely a magnitude/off-distribution effect, and prior adaptive-strength methods (STU-PID) already suffice. The elaboration direction may be inseparable from a raw difficulty direction; we control by comparing predictive gain over a difficulty probe and by using correctness-conditioned contrasts. Projecting the brevity direction into a subspace may itself push activations off-distribution and reduce compression benefit, narrowing the Pareto gain; we sweep and report validity. Difficulty stratification for open math is noisy; we use empirical pass-rates and benchmark-provided levels. Reproducing FlowSteer/other baselines may be infeasible in an academic budget; we prioritize ASC, Efficiency Steering, and STU-PID as the core comparisons. Results may be model-family and scale specific, and the entanglement magnitude may vary with instruction/RL tuning. Reasoning-length measurement requires clear think/answer delimiters."}}
