n, and (iii) predicts local fragility above and beyond a difficulty probe and a confidence probe (principal-angle separability + joint-model predictive gain). 5) Causal validation (Causal Intervention): Use activation patching to identify components carrying the engagement signal; patch an EARLY high-engagement state into a LATE step position and test whether (a) the model produces more verification and (b) an injected late error is more often caught/corrected (fragility drops). Controls: matched-random-direction patch, off-target accuracy preserved; report sign, dose, specificity. 6) Training-free reallocation intervention: Derive a verification-engagement steering direction (difference-of-means between high- and low-engagement step states). During generation, steer engagement upward on the late portion of the chain (e.g., last 20-30% by predicted position, or when a position probe fires). Evaluate on GSM8K, MATH500, AIME, GPQA. Metrics: accuracy (overall and on items where errors would otherwise slip through late), added tokens, tokens-at-fixed-accuracy, late-fragility reduction (re-run error injection under the intervention). Baselines: no intervention, uniform verification boosting across all positions (should cost more tokens for less gain), external position-weighted correction (ASCoT-style), and prompting ('carefully re-check your final steps'). Key claim: targeted late-step engagement steering repairs fragility more token-efficiently than uniform boosting or external pipelines. 7) Failure/specificity analysis: verify boosting late verification does not induce harmful oscillation/underthinking on easy items (measure over-correction rate) and that early verification is not degraded. 8) Generalization: transfer the engagement probe and steering direction across datasets and model scales without retraining; test whether the gradient's steepness correlates with fragility severity across models.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': "Verification-event detection is noisy (lexical + classifier); we validate against human-labeled steps and report agreement, and use two detection methods for robustness. The effort decay could be legitimately justified if late steps are genuinely easier or less error-prone; the difficulty-control and residual-work analyses address this, and a null after controls (decay is difficulty-driven) would be an informative negative result. Error-injection fragility is sensitive to how 'matched' injected errors are across positions; we use templated, magnitude-matched perturbations with LLM-judge coherence checks and report per-error-type results. The verification-engagement signal may be inseparable from a generic uncertainty/confidence direction; the separability analysis and causal patching guard against this, and overlap would still be a mechanistic finding. Steering late engagement could trigger over-verification/oscillation (added tokens or new errors); the specificity and over-correction analyses and dose sweeps quantify this trade-off. Reasoning-length/position measurement requires clear think/answer delimiters; error injection and truncate-and-resume are compute-heavy, so we subsample. The intervention may not beat strong external correction baselines on accuracy alone, but the phenomenon (verification effort anti-aligned with fragility) and its decodable, steerable internal cause are standalone contributions. Effects may be model-family and scale specific, and the direction of the gradient could vary across architectures (itself a publishable characterization)."}

