Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "dimensional_consistency_blindspot", "Title": "Right Number, Wrong Unit: An Internal Dimension Representation That Reasoning Models Encode But Fail to Consult", "Short Hypothesis": "Reasoning models internally encode the physical DIMENSION/unit-type of each quantity they manipulate (length vs time vs mass vs concentration), separately from its magnitude, but frequently fail to consult this representation at the moment a dimensionally-inconsistent operation is committed (adding incompatible units, dropping a conversion, reporting the wrong unit). This know-but-dont-check gap is decodable, causally real, and most dangerous in safety-critical domains like medication dosing; a cheap dimensional-consistency monitor built from the internal representation catches unit errors the model itself misses.", "Related Work": "Recent interpretability work shows LLMs linearly encode numerical LOG-MAGNITUDE (LLMs Know More About Numbers than They Can Say 2026; Number Representations as Human Mental Number Line 2025) and that irrelevant numerical context perturbs these magnitude representations (Interpreting Multi-Attribute Confounding 2025; Probing for Arithmetic Errors 2025). All of this concerns MAGNITUDE (how big a number is) and arithmetic correctness, never the physical DIMENSION/unit-TYPE of a quantity or dimensional consistency of operations. Separately, a large human-science education literature documents that unit/dimensional-analysis errors are pervasive and catastrophic in physics, engineering, and medicine (Using Math in Physics: Dimensional Analysis 2021; Check Your Work: Dimensional Homogeneity 2023; Metric Unit Conversion 2018), and that dimensional homogeneity is a powerful, underused error check. No prior work asks whether LLMs maintain an internal dimension representation, whether they use it to enforce dimensional consistency, or where dimensional errors originate mechanistically. We transfer the human-science phenomenon of dimensional-blindness into LLMs and into the highest-stakes domain (medication dosing), reframing the known know-but-cant-say magnitude gap as a know-but-dont-CHECK gap for units. This is a new phenomenon (dimensional-consistency blindness), a new internal representation (unit-type, distinct from magnitude), and a safety-critical decision-auditing angle, distinct from all prior magnitude/arithmetic probing.", "Abstract": "Human students and even engineers routinely make dimensional errors\u2014adding a length to a time, forgetting a unit conversion, reporting an answer in the wrong unit\u2014and dimensional-consistency checking is a classic remedy. We ask whether reasoning language models exhibit the same blind spot and, mechanistically, why. We hypothesize that models internally encode the physical DIMENSION (unit-type: length, time, mass, concentration, dose-per-mass, etc.) of each quantity they handle, separately from its numerical magnitude, but frequently fail to consult this representation at the point where a dimensionally-inconsistent operation is committed. We test this on templated unit-conversion and multi-step physics/engineering word problems, and\u2014where the stakes are highest\u2014on medication-dosing scenarios (mg vs mg/kg vs mL, unit-conversion traps) using open reasoning models (DeepSeek-R1 distills 1.5B-14B, Qwen3). We first quantify a dimensional-error rate and show it is distinct from magnitude/arithmetic errors: models often compute the right number but attach or combine the wrong units. We then train lightweight linear probes at reasoning-step boundaries to decode the unit-type of the current working quantity, test that this dimension representation is separable from the magnitude representation, and show it is often internally correct even on steps where the model commits a dimensional error\u2014the know-but-dont-check gap. We localize the representation and causally validate it via activation patching (patch the correct unit-type in and observe the error avoided) and steering. Finally we build a training-free dimensional-consistency monitor that reads the internal unit-type at each step and flags incompatible operations, catching dosing errors the model misses at low token cost. Our work reveals a safety-critical dimensional blind spot and traces it to an unconsulted internal representation.", "Experiments": "1) Dimensional-error phenomenon (behavioral): Construct three problem sets with ground-truth unit annotations: (a) templated unit-conversion chains (e.g., km/h -> m/s, nested prefix conversions), (b) multi-step physics/engineering word problems where intermediate quantities have known dimensions and a correct final unit, and (c) medication-dosing scenarios (compute a dose given weight in kg, concentration in mg/mL, dose in mg/kg; include unit-conversion traps like g<->mg, mL<->L). Generate reasoning traces on DeepSeek-R1 distills (1.5B/7B/8B/14B) and Qwen3. Classify each error as DIMENSIONAL (wrong unit-type / incompatible combination / missing conversion / wrong final unit) vs MAGNITUDE/ARITHMETIC (right units, wrong number) via parsing + LLM-judge with human spot-checks. Metrics: dimensional-error rate, and crucially the fraction of dimensional errors where the numerical computation is otherwise correct (right number, wrong unit). Report especially for dosing (safety stakes). 2) Unit-type probing and separability from magnitude (Location): Segment traces into steps. At each step boundary, extract residual-stream activations for the current working quantity and train a linear probe to classify its unit-type (multi-class: length/time/mass/volume/concentration/dose-per-mass/dimensionless/...). Validate AUROC/accuracy, layer sweep. Test separability from a magnitude probe (reproduce a log-magnitude regressor) via principal angles between the unit-type subspace and the magnitude direction and via joint-model predictive gain\u2014establishing dimension is a distinct representation, not a magnitude artifact. 3) Know-but-dont-check test (core): On steps where the model commits a dimensional error, read the unit-type probe on the INPUT quantities just before the erroneous operation. Hypothesis: the internal unit-types are often correct even though the operation the model then performs is dimensionally invalid\u2014quantifying the gap between internally-available dimension info and its non-use. Compare probe accuracy on error-committing vs correct steps. 4) Causal validation (Causal Intervention): Localize the unit-type representation via activation patching; at an about-to-err step, patch in the correct unit-type representation (from a matched correct-unit context) and test whether the model avoids the dimensional error (produces a compatible operation / correct final unit). Controls: matched-random-direction patch, off-target accuracy preserved; report sign, dose-response, specificity. Also steer along a unit-type direction to induce/suppress a target unit. 5) Dimensional-consistency monitor (training-free intervention): At each step boundary, read the unit-type probe on the operands; when an operation combines incompatible dimensions or the running dimension diverges from the target answer's dimension, flag it (and optionally trigger a re-derivation or unit-check prompt). Evaluate on all three sets, with emphasis on dosing. Metrics: dimensional-error catch rate (recall/precision of flagged errors), corrected-answer rate after re-derivation, added tokens, and false-flag rate on correct traces. Baselines: no monitor, self-verification prompting ('check your units'), an arithmetic-error probe (Probing for Arithmetic Errors) repurposed, and an LLM-judge post-hoc checker. Key claim: the internal-representation monitor catches unit/dosing errors the model misses more cheaply and reliably than prompting or post-hoc checking. 6) Safety focus: Report catch rate specifically on medication-dosing unit errors and characterize the most dangerous residual failure modes (e.g., 10x dosing errors from g/mg confusion). 7) Generalization: transfer probe and monitor across datasets and model scales without retraining; test whether larger models both encode dimension better AND consult it more (does the know-but-dont-check gap shrink with scale?).", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "Identifying the 'current working quantity' and annotating ground-truth unit-types at each step is nontrivial; we mitigate with templated problems (known dimensions by construction), parsing plus LLM-judge, and human validation with reported agreement. The unit-type representation may be inseparable from magnitude or from surface token identity of the unit string (a probe could just read the literal 'mg' token rather than an abstract dimension); we control by testing generalization across notations/synonyms (mg vs milligram), across problems where the unit token is not adjacent, and via the separability analysis and causal patching\u2014if the signal is purely lexical that is an informative negative result. Dimensional errors may be rarer in strong models, limiting statistical power (itself a boundary-condition finding on the know-but-dont-check gap vs scale). The patching intervention could push activations off-distribution and introduce new errors; matched-random-direction controls and validity checks are included. The monitor may over-flag on legitimately dimensionless or context-dependent quantities (e.g., ratios), inflating false positives; we tune thresholds and report precision. Medication-dosing scenarios are synthetic and should not be treated as clinical validation\u2014they demonstrate the failure mode, not a deployable clinical tool. Results may be model-family and scale specific, and reasoning-step segmentation requires clear delimiters."}}
