Action: FinalizeIdea
Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "confidence_contagion_carryover", "Title": "Confidence Bleeds Across Questions: A Carried-Over Certainty State That Corrupts Calibration on Later Sub-Questions", "Short Hypothesis": "When a model answers several sub-questions within a single response, its expressed and internal confidence on a later, weakly-known sub-question is contaminated by the certainty it carried from an earlier, strongly-known sub-question answered in the same context. Confidence is not reset per sub-question: a linearly-decodable certainty state persists across answer boundaries, so a confidently-correct first answer inflates confidence (and worsens calibration) on a subsequent shaky one. This carry-over is causally real and can be cancelled by resetting the certainty representation at each sub-question boundary.", "Related Work": "Confidence/calibration interpretability studies confidence for a single answer or fact: a linear Verbal Uncertainty Feature (VUF, 2025) governs verbalized uncertainty and can be steered to reduce hallucinations; 'Confidence is Not Competence' (2025) shows a decodable but causally-inert solvability belief; SCIURus (2025) finds shared uncertainty/factuality circuits; TracVC (2026) shows confidence is often grounded in superficial certainty cues; 'Read Your Own Mind' (2025) shows verbal confidence is a statistic of the sampled distribution. All of these treat each answer's confidence in isolation and never ask whether confidence STATE CARRIES OVER across multiple answers within one response. Separately, sycophancy and knowledge-conflict work studies belief change under external pressure, not within-response self-contamination. My prior proposals concern reasoning efficiency and framing effects, never confidence carry-over. We are the first to (a) demonstrate a within-response confidence contagion phenomenon (a strong first answer inflates confidence and degrades calibration on a later weak one), (b) tie it to a persistent, non-resetting certainty representation, and (c) causally cancel it by resetting that representation at sub-question boundaries. This reframes miscalibration as a state-management failure across answer boundaries, distinct from all per-answer confidence work, and reuses the known VUF direction as a candidate carrier.", "Abstract": "Large language models are widely used to answer multi-part questions in a single response \u2014 a differential diagnosis followed by a dosing recommendation, a summary followed by a numeric estimate. Confidence-calibration research has almost entirely studied the confidence of a single, isolated answer. We ask a different question: within one response, does the confidence a model carries from an earlier sub-question it knows well contaminate its confidence on a later sub-question it knows poorly? We hypothesize confidence contagion: a confidently-correct first answer inflates both expressed and internal confidence on a subsequent shaky answer, worsening its calibration, because the model maintains a certainty state that does not reset at answer boundaries. We construct paired multi-sub-question prompts in which a target weakly-known sub-question is preceded either by a strongly-known (high-confidence) sub-question or by a matched neutral/weak one, holding the target identical. Across factual QA, math, and \u2014 for the highest stakes \u2014 clinical vignettes, on open models (Llama-3.1-8B, Qwen3, Gemma2-9B, and reasoning distills), we measure whether the target's verbalized confidence, token-probability confidence, and calibration error shift with the predecessor's confidence, independent of target difficulty. Mechanistically, we test whether a linearly-decodable certainty state (reusing the Verbal Uncertainty Feature direction) persists across the answer boundary and predicts the contamination, localize it, causally validate via activation patching (patch a low-certainty state into a high-carry-over context and observe calibration recover), and deploy a training-free boundary-reset intervention. Confidence contagion is a safety concern: a model made overconfident on a dangerous recommendation by an unrelated prior success.", "Experiments": "1) Establish contagion (behavioral): Build paired prompts asking two (and later k) sub-questions in one response. Fix the TARGET as a weakly-known item (empirically low accuracy for the model) and vary only the PREDECESSOR: (a) a strongly-known item the model answers confidently and correctly, (b) a matched weakly-known item, (c) a neutral filler question. Datasets: factual QA (TriviaQA/PopQA hard-vs-easy splits), math (easy GSM8K predecessor + hard MATH target), and clinical vignettes (a confidently-answerable perception/recall question followed by a dosing/management question). Elicit per-sub-question verbalized confidence (0-100) and record token-probability confidence and correctness. Metrics: target verbalized confidence, internal confidence, and Expected Calibration Error (ECE) as a function of predecessor condition. Hypothesis: high-confidence predecessor inflates target confidence and worsens target ECE vs. neutral/weak predecessors. Report within-target effect sizes.\n2) Dissociation from target difficulty and position: Mixed-effects model of target confidence/ECE with random effect for target identity (intrinsic difficulty) and fixed effect for predecessor condition; test significance after accounting for target identity. Control for context length/position with a matched-length neutral filler; vary predecessor-target ordering to rule out generic position/recency effects (does the effect require a CONFIDENT predecessor, not just any predecessor?).\n3) Certainty-state persistence probing (Location): At the TARGET answer position, extract residual-stream activations and (i) reproduce a linear certainty/Verbal-Uncertainty-Feature probe, (ii) train a probe predicting target verbalized confidence. Test whether, for identical targets, the certainty-state readout shifts with predecessor confidence \u2014 i.e., certainty carries across the answer boundary. Layer sweep for localization; test separability from a target-difficulty probe via principal angles and joint-model predictive gain.\n4) Contamination predicts miscalibration: Regress target overconfidence (verbalized minus empirical accuracy) on the carried-over certainty-state magnitude vs. baselines (predecessor lexical features, target difficulty probe, position). Compare R^2.\n5) Causal validation (Causal Intervention): Use activation patching to locate components carrying the certainty state; patch the target-position certainty state from a neutral-predecessor context into a high-confidence-predecessor context (same target) and test whether target confidence and ECE recover. Controls: matched-random-direction patch; off-target (predecessor) answer preserved; report sign, dose-response, specificity. Reverse patch (inject high certainty) to confirm bidirectionality.\n6) Boundary-reset intervention (training-free): At each sub-question boundary, steer the certainty-state representation toward a neutral baseline (subtract the carried-over VUF component) before the target is answered. Evaluate on all datasets. Baselines: no intervention, prompt-based reset ('assess this question independently'), MUC-style VUF calibration applied per-answer, and asking each sub-question in a SEPARATE conversation (oracle upper bound on decontamination). Metrics: target ECE, overconfidence rate, accuracy (must not drop), and in clinical vignettes the rate of overconfident dangerous recommendations.\n7) Modulators & generalization: vary number of preceding confident answers (does contagion accumulate?), predecessor correctness (does a confidently-WRONG predecessor also contaminate?), and domain relatedness (does an unrelated-domain confident predecessor still bleed in?). Transfer probe/reset across datasets and model scales without retraining; test reasoning distills vs. base instruct models.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "The effect could be a generic position/recency or context-length artifact rather than confidence-specific; the confident-vs-weak-vs-neutral predecessor design, matched-length filler, and ordering controls isolate this, and a null after controls is an informative negative result. Confidence elicitation (verbalized 0-100) is noisy and prompt-sensitive; we use multiple elicitation formats and cross-check with token-probability confidence. The carried-over certainty state may be inseparable from a generic difficulty or topic-salience direction; the separability analysis, causal patching, and reuse of the established VUF direction address this, and overlap with VUF would be a positive mechanistic finding. Contagion may be weak in strongly instruction/RL-tuned models trained to answer each item independently (a valuable boundary condition). Steering/patching can push activations off-distribution and harm accuracy; matched-random-direction controls and accuracy checks are included, and the boundary reset must not suppress legitimately high confidence (specificity check on strongly-known targets). Clinical vignettes are synthetic demonstrations of the failure mode, not clinical validation. Effects may be model-family and scale specific, and building matched strong/weak sub-question pairs per model requires per-model calibration of item difficulty."}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'confidence_contagion_carryover', 'Title': 'Confidence Bleeds Across Questions: A Carried-Over Certainty State That Corrupts Calibration on Later Sub-Questions', 'Short Hypothesis': 'When a model answers several sub-questions within a single response, its expressed and internal confidence on a later, weakly-known sub-question is contaminated by the certainty it carried from an earlier, strongly-known sub-question answered in the same context. Confidence is not reset per sub-question: a linearly-decodable certainty state persists across answer boundaries, so a confidently-correct first answer inflates confidence (and worsens calibration) on a subsequent shaky one. This carry-over is causally real and can be cancelled by resetting the certainty representation at each sub-question boundary.', 'Related Work': "Confidence/calibration interpretability studies confidence for a single answer or fact: a linear Verbal Uncertainty Feature (VUF, 2025) governs verbalized uncertainty and can be steered to reduce hallucinations; 'Confidence is Not Competence' (2025) shows a decodable but causally-inert solvability belief; SCIURus (2025) finds shared uncertainty/factuality circuits; TracVC (2026) shows confidence is often grounded in superficial certainty cues; 'Read Your Own Mind' (2025) shows verbal confidence is a statistic of the sampled distribution. All of these treat each answer's confidence in isolation and never ask whether confidence STATE CARRIES OVER across multiple answers within one response. Separately, sycophancy and knowledge-conflict work studies belief change under external pressure, not within-response self-contamination. My prior proposals concern reasoning efficiency and framing effects, never confidence carry-over. We are the first to (a) demonstrate a within-response confidence contagion phenomenon (a strong first answer inflates confidence and degrades calibration on a later weak one), (b) tie it to a persistent, non-resetting certainty representation, and (c) causally cancel it by resetting that representation at sub-question boundaries. This reframes miscalibration as a state-management failure across answer boundaries, distinct from all per-answer confidence work, and reuses the known VUF direction as a candidate carrier.", 'Abstract': "Large language models are widely used to answer multi-part questions in a single response — a differential diagnosis followed by a dosing recommendation, a summary followed by a numeric estimate. Confidence-calibration research has almost entirely studied the confidence of a single, isolated answer. We ask a different question: within one response, does the confidence a model carries from an earlier sub-question it knows well contaminate its confidence on a later sub-question it knows poorly? We hypothesize confidence contagion: a confidently-correct first answer inflates both expressed and internal confidence on a subsequent shaky answer, worsening its calibration, because the model maintains a certainty state that does not reset at answer boundaries. We construct paired multi-sub-question prompts in which a target weakly-known sub-question is preceded either by a strongly-known (high-confidence) sub-question or by a matched neutral/weak one, holding the target identical. Across factual QA, math, and — for the highest stakes — clinical vignettes, on open models (Llama-3.1-8B, Qwen3, Gemma2-9B, and reasoning distills), we measure whether the target's verbalized confidence, token-probability confidence, and calibration error shift with the predecessor's confidence, independent of target difficulty. Mechanistically, we test whether a linearly-decodable certainty state (reusing the Verbal Uncertainty Feature direction) persists across the answer boundary and predicts the contamination, localize it, causally validate via activation patching (patch a low-certainty state into a high-carry-over context and observe calibration recover), and deploy a training-free boundary-reset intervention. Confidence contagion is a safety concern: a model made overconfident on a dangerous recommendation by an unrelated prior success.", 'Experiments': "1) Establish contagion (behavioral): Build paired prompts asking two (and later k) sub-questions in one response. Fix the TARGET as a weakly-known item (empirically low accuracy for the model) and vary only the PREDECESSOR: (a) a strongly-known item the model answers confidently and correctly, (b) a matched weakly-known item, (c) a neutral filler question. Datasets: factual QA (TriviaQA/PopQA hard-vs-easy splits), math (easy GSM8K predecessor + hard MATH target), and clinical vignettes (a confidently-answerable perception/recall question followed by a dosing/management question). Elicit per-sub-question verbalized confidence (0-100) and record token-probability confidence and correctness. Metrics: target verbalized confidence, internal confidence, and Expected Calibration Error (ECE) as a function of predecessor condition. Hypothesis: high-confidence predecessor inflates target confidence and worsens target ECE vs. neutral/weak predecessors. Report within-target effect sizes.\n2) Dissociation from target difficulty and position: Mixed-effects model of target confidence/ECE with random effect for target identity (intrinsic difficulty) and fixed effect for predecessor condition; test significance after accounting for target identity. Control for context length/position with a matched-length neutral filler; vary predecessor-target ordering to rule out generic position/recency effects (does the effect require a CONFIDENT predecessor, not just any predecessor?).\n3) Certainty-state persistence probing (Location): At the TARGET answer position, extract residual-stream activations and (i) reproduce a linear certainty/Verbal-Uncertainty-Feature probe, (ii) train a probe predicting target verbalized confidence. Test whether, for identical targets, the certainty-state readout shifts with predecessor confidence — i.e., certainty carries across the answer boundary. Layer sweep for localization; test separability from a target-difficulty probe via principal angles and joint-model predictive gain.\n4) Contamination predicts miscalibration: Regress target overconfidence (verbalized minus empirical accuracy) on the carried-over certainty-state magnitude vs. baselines (predecessor lexical features, target difficulty probe, position). Compare R^2.\n5) Causal validation (Causal Intervention): Use activation patching to locate components carrying the certainty state; patch the target-position certainty state from a neutral-predecessor context into a high-confidence-predecessor context (same target) and test whether target confidence and ECE recover. Controls: matched-random-direction patch; off-target (predecessor) answer preserved; report sign, dose-response, specificity. Reverse patch (inject high certainty) to confirm bidirectionality.\n6) Boundary-reset intervention (training-free): At each sub-question boundary, steer the certainty-state representation toward a neutral baseline (subtract the carried-over VUF component) before the target is answered. Evaluate on all datasets. Baselines: no intervention, prompt-based reset ('assess this question independently'), MUC-style VUF calibration applied per-answer, and asking each sub-question in a SEPARATE conversation (oracle upper bound on decontamination). Metrics: target ECE, overconfidence rate, accuracy (must not drop), and in clinical vignettes the rate of overconfident dangerous recommendations.\n7) Modulators & generalization: vary number of preceding confident answers (does contagion accumulate?), predecessor correctness (does a confidently-WRONG predecessor also contaminate?), and domain relatedness (does an unrelated-domain confident predecessor still bleed in?). Transfer probe/reset across datasets and model scales without retraining; test reasoning distills vs. base instruct models.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': 'The effect could be a generic position/recency or context-length artifact rather than confidence-specific; the confident-vs-weak-vs-neutral predecessor design, matched-length filler, and ordering controls isolate this, and a null after controls is an informative negative result. Confidence elicitation (verbalized 0-100) is noisy and prompt-sensitive; we use multiple elicitation formats and cross-check with token-probability confidence. The carried-over certainty state may be inseparable from a generic difficulty or topic-salience direction; the separability analysis, causal patching, and reuse of the established VUF direction address this, and overlap with VUF would be a positive mechanistic finding. Contagion may be weak in strongly instruction/RL-tuned models trained to answer each item independently (a valuable boundary condition). Steering/patching can push activations off-distribution and harm accuracy; matched-random-direction controls and accuracy checks are included, and the boundary reset must not suppress legitimately high confidence (specificity check on strongly-known targets). Clinical vignettes are synthetic demonstrations of the failure mode, not clinical validation. Effects may be model-family and scale specific, and building matched strong/weak sub-question pairs per model requires per-model calibration of item difficulty.'}

