Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "context_complexity_difficulty_contamination", "Title": "Guilty by Association: How Benign Surrounding Context Inflates a Model's Internal Difficulty Estimate and Wastes Reasoning Compute", "Short Hypothesis": "The pre-generation difficulty representation that reasoning models use to allocate thinking compute is contaminated by the complexity of BENIGN, task-irrelevant surrounding context: embedding the exact same target question inside a longer, more elaborate but irrelevant passage (as real users and RAG systems routinely do) raises the model's internal difficulty estimate and inflates reasoning tokens on the target, independent of the target's true difficulty. This contamination is decodable at the prompt boundary, causally real, and cancelable by resetting the difficulty representation.", "Related Work": "Difficulty-perception work (Probing the Difficulty Perception Mechanism, 2025) shows difficulty is linearly encoded in final-layer heads and drives adaptive compute, but only on clean, isolated problems. Overthinking-cause work attributes waste to internal bias/first-impression (2025), missing premises (MiP-Overthinking, 2025), or a length/verbosity direction, all with the target question in isolation. Distractor/attack work (Distractor Injection Attacks, 2025; CODE, 2026; contextual entrainment, 2025; LeaF confounding tokens, 2025; Don't Take Things Out of Context, 2025) studies ADVERSARIAL or spurious tokens that divert attention or are maliciously optimized to inflate tokens. Crucially, none studies BENIGN, plausible, non-adversarial surrounding context (the kind real users and retrieval systems naturally include) as a contaminant of the model's DIFFICULTY ESTIMATE that inflates reasoning compute on an embedded target. We are the first to (a) demonstrate benign context-complexity contamination of reasoning compute on matched targets, (b) tie it specifically to the pre-generation difficulty representation (reusing the known difficulty direction/heads) rather than to attention-diversion or attack mechanisms, and (c) causally reset the difficulty representation to cancel it. This is distinct from adversarial distractor work (mechanism and benign framing), from clean-prompt difficulty-perception work (contamination not studied), and from all my prior overthinking proposals (none address context-driven difficulty contamination).", "Abstract": "Reasoning models allocate a thinking budget that is assumed to reflect a problem's genuine difficulty, and difficulty is known to be linearly encoded in the model's activations before it reasons. But real questions rarely arrive in isolation: users paste a question into a long email, a chat has scrollback, and retrieval-augmented systems prepend passages. We ask whether such benign, task-irrelevant surrounding context contaminates the model's internal difficulty estimate and inflates reasoning compute on the embedded target question, even when the target's true difficulty is unchanged. We construct matched prompts that hold a math or science target question and its correct answer fixed while varying only the surrounding, clearly-irrelevant benign context (none, short-simple, long-elaborate) that a plausible user might include. Across GSM8K, MATH500, AIME, and GPQA on open reasoning models (DeepSeek-R1 distills 1.5B-14B, Qwen3), we measure reasoning-token count and accuracy on the target as a function of surrounding-context complexity, using a mixed-effects decomposition to dissociate context complexity from target difficulty and from raw prompt length. Mechanistically, we reproduce a linear difficulty probe at the prompt boundary and test whether, for identical targets, its readout rises with irrelevant-context complexity; we localize the difficulty representation and causally validate via activation patching (patch a no-context difficulty state into a high-context prompt and observe the budget drop) and steering. Finally we deploy a training-free difficulty-reset intervention that cancels the contamination, cutting tokens at equal accuracy. Because messy, benign context is the norm in deployment and RAG, this reveals a pervasive, non-adversarial compute tax with a decodable, steerable internal cause.", "Experiments": "1) Establish context-complexity contamination (behavioral): For each target question (GSM8K, MATH500, AIME, GPQA), create content-identical variants that embed the SAME target inside surrounding benign, clearly-irrelevant context of increasing complexity: (a) no context (isolated), (b) short-simple context (one plain sentence of unrelated small talk), (c) long-elaborate context (several sentences of unrelated but syntactically/lexically complex material, e.g. an intricate but off-topic narrative). Keep the target text and correct answer fixed and mark the target clearly ('Please answer this question: ...'). Generate reasoning traces per variant. Metrics: reasoning-token count and accuracy on the target per condition. Hypothesis: reasoning tokens rise monotonically with surrounding-context complexity at equal accuracy. Report within-target effect sizes. 2) Dissociation from target difficulty and prompt length: Fit a mixed-effects model of reasoning length with random effect for target identity (intrinsic difficulty) and fixed effects for surrounding-context complexity and raw prompt token-length (as a covariate). Test for a significant context-complexity effect after accounting for target identity AND prompt length; quantify variance attributable to context complexity vs difficulty vs length. Include a length-matched control where short-simple context is padded to the token length of long-elaborate context with trivial repetition, isolating COMPLEXITY from LENGTH. 3) Wasted vs genuine compute: Use truncate-and-resume to estimate minimal-solve length per variant; test whether context complexity inflates WASTED tokens (beyond minimal solve) and re-reading of irrelevant material (via attention-to-context and lexical restatement tagging) rather than genuine extra reasoning on the target. 4) Location - contaminated difficulty probe: Reproduce a linear difficulty probe on prompt-boundary (last prompt token, before any reasoning token) activations, trained on isolated problems of graded difficulty. Apply it to the matched variants and test whether, for identical targets, the probe-decoded difficulty rises with irrelevant-context complexity. Also train a probe predicting reasoning length and test the same shift. Compare against a true-difficulty ground truth (target pass-rate) to show the internal estimate is inflated relative to actual difficulty. Layer sweep and (following prior work) test the difficulty-sensitive final-layer heads. 5) Causal validation (Causal Intervention): Patch the prompt-boundary residual state (and/or the difficulty-head activations) from the no-context variant into the matched high-context prompt (same target) and measure whether reasoning length drops toward the no-context level. Controls: matched-random-direction patch; off-target accuracy preserved. Report sign, dose-response, specificity. Reverse patch (no-context -> high-context) to confirm bidirectionality. 6) Difficulty-reset intervention (training-free): Derive a difficulty steering direction (difference-of-means between high-context and no-context boundary states, or the difficulty-probe weight) and steer high-context prompts toward the no-context difficulty level at problem onset. Evaluate on all datasets. Metrics: reduction in context-driven length inflation, tokens-at-equal-accuracy, wall-clock speedup, accuracy retention. Baselines: no intervention, prompt-based fix ('ignore surrounding context; answer only the question'), context-stripping preprocessing (oracle upper bound), fixed truncation. 7) RAG realism: Test with realistic retrieved-but-irrelevant passages prepended (as in RAG) to show the effect holds for genuinely deployment-shaped context, not just synthetic small talk. 8) Generalization: transfer probe and reset across datasets and model scales without retraining; test whether the contamination grows with context complexity and whether larger/RL-tuned models are more or less robust.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "The effect could be a raw prompt-length artifact rather than complexity per se; the length-matched padded control and the prompt-length covariate in the mixed-effects model isolate complexity, and a null after controls (pure length effect) would itself be an informative, publishable finding. The surrounding context must be genuinely benign and irrelevant, not a covert distractor; we verify irrelevance and use plain, non-adversarial material plus realistic RAG passages, distinguishing this from adversarial distractor-injection work. The contaminated difficulty signal may be inseparable from a generic attention-diversion or contextual-entrainment effect; we control by testing whether the difficulty probe/heads (not just attention) shift, comparing to an entrainment-head baseline, and via causal patching of the difficulty representation specifically. Steering/patching can push activations off-distribution and confound accuracy reads; matched-random-direction controls and sample-validity checks are included, and resetting difficulty must not harm targets that are genuinely hard (specificity check on hard isolated targets). The effect may be small in strongly instruction/RL-tuned models trained to focus on the marked question (a valuable boundary condition). Reasoning-length measurement requires clear think/answer delimiters; truncate-and-resume is compute-heavy, so we subsample. Results may be model-family and scale specific."}}
