Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "isolation_induced_overthinking", "Title": "Alone With the Problem: Isolation-Induced Overthinking and the Attention-Fixation Mechanism Behind It", "Short Hypothesis": "The exact same problem induces substantially more overthinking (reasoning tokens, self-doubt loops) when presented alone than when embedded among unrelated peer problems in the same context, and this isolation-induced overthinking is causally driven by the model over-allocating attention to its lone input question. Diluting that attention\u2014by presenting the problem among peers or by directly capping attention-to-own-question\u2014cuts tokens without hurting accuracy.", "Related Work": "Two observations motivate this. (1) Batch prompting (Srivastava et al. 2025) empirically reduces reasoning tokens by ~76% at preserved accuracy when queries share a context, attributing it loosely to reduced per-query effort, pattern induction, and hedging suppression\u2014but offers NO mechanistic account and does not isolate WHICH effect matters or WHY effort drops. (2) The First Impression Problem (2025) shows overthinking is triggered by an internal bias from the input question, mechanistically via EXCESSIVE ATTENTION to the input question, and shows removing the question mid-reasoning reduces redundant reasoning. These two are unconnected. We unify them: we hypothesize batch prompting works precisely because embedding a problem among peers DILUTES the model's attention to its own question, defusing the first-impression bias that drives overthinking. Distinct from batch-prompting work (which is behavioral and treats batching as a throughput trick), from First-Impression work (which studies a single isolated problem and never varies context composition), and from all my prior overthinking proposals (which study within-single-problem internal dynamics under a fixed, isolated prompt). Our contribution: a new phenomenon (isolation vs peer-context overthinking on matched problems), a mechanistic account (attention-fixation on own question), and a causal, training-free intervention (attention dilution / capping).", "Abstract": "Reasoning models overthink\u2014but almost all study of this assumes a problem is presented alone, exactly the format that may most inflate it. We identify a new phenomenon: isolation-induced overthinking. The identical problem, presented alone, provokes substantially more reasoning tokens and more self-doubt loops than when it is embedded among unrelated peer problems in the same context, even though its content, difficulty, and correct answer are unchanged. We further propose a mechanism unifying two disconnected findings\u2014that batch prompting cheaply suppresses overthinking, and that overthinking is triggered by excessive attention to the input question: we hypothesize that peer context works by DILUTING the model's attention to its own question, defusing the first-impression bias that drives redundant reflection. Across GSM8K, MATH500, AIME, and GPQA on open reasoning models (DeepSeek-R1 distills 1.5B-14B, Qwen3), we hold the target problem fixed and vary only the number and nature of surrounding peer problems, measuring per-target reasoning tokens, self-doubt behavior, and accuracy. Mechanistically, we measure attention mass directed at the target's own question tokens as a function of peer count, test whether it predicts the overthinking reduction, localize the attention-fixation heads, and causally validate by directly capping attention-to-own-question during isolated generation\u2014testing whether this reproduces the peer-context token savings without peers. We compare against batch prompting and length steering. Our work reveals that how a problem is packaged silently sets how hard a model overthinks it, and traces this to a manipulable attention-fixation mechanism.", "Experiments": "1) Establish isolation-induced overthinking (behavioral): For each TARGET problem (GSM8K, MATH500, AIME, GPQA), create matched contexts that present the SAME target either (a) alone, or (b) embedded among N unrelated peer problems (N in {1,2,4,8}) drawn from a different topic/difficulty, all answered in one response, with the target's position randomized. Keep the target text and correct answer fixed. Measure, on the target only: reasoning-token count, number of self-doubt/hedging events ('wait', 'let me double-check', 'actually'; via lexical cues + a reflection-direction classifier), and accuracy. Hypothesis: reasoning tokens on the target decrease monotonically with peer count at preserved accuracy. Report within-target effect sizes and dose-response over N. Include an 'irrelevant filler text' control (padding of matched length that is NOT peer problems) to test whether the effect requires PEER PROBLEMS specifically, not mere context length.\n2) Dissociation from length/position confounds: Fit a mixed-effects model of target reasoning length with random effect for target identity (difficulty) and fixed effects for peer count and raw context token-length (covariate). Test the peer-count effect after accounting for target identity and length; use the length-matched filler control to isolate peer-problem presence from prompt length. Vary target position (first vs middle vs last) to rule out pure serial-position effects.\n3) Wasted vs genuine compute: Use truncate-and-resume to estimate minimal-solve length; test whether peer context reduces WASTED tokens (beyond minimal solve) and self-doubt loops rather than genuine reasoning. Confirm accuracy is preserved or improved (as batch-prompting work reports).\n4) Attention-fixation mechanism (Location): During generation of the target's reasoning, measure the attention mass directed at the TARGET's own question tokens, as a function of peer count. Hypothesis: attention-to-own-question is highest in isolation and dilutes as peers are added, and its magnitude predicts the overthinking level (regress target reasoning tokens on attention-to-own-question vs peer count and difficulty; compare R^2). Localize the specific heads/layers whose attention-to-own-question best tracks overthinking (head attribution), connecting to the First-Impression excessive-attention mechanism.\n5) Causal validation (Causal Intervention): During ISOLATED generation (no peers), directly cap/downweight attention to the target's own question tokens on the identified fixation heads (attention masking / rescaling), and test whether this reproduces the peer-context token savings and self-doubt reduction WITHOUT any peers. Controls: cap attention on matched-random heads (no effect), cap attention to non-question tokens (specificity), off-target accuracy preserved. Report sign, dose-response (cap strength), and specificity. Reverse test: artificially amplify attention-to-own-question in a peer context and check overthinking returns.\n6) Comparison and application: Compare the attention-cap intervention and peer-context (batch) prompting against no intervention, length/verbosity steering (Efficiency Steering), and fixed truncation. Metrics: accuracy (overall and hardest strata), mean tokens, tokens-at-equal-accuracy, self-doubt-event reduction, wall-clock. Also quantify the practical implication: how much do isolated single-problem benchmark protocols OVER-estimate a model's overthinking relative to realistic multi-problem/batched deployment.\n7) Generalization: transfer the attention-cap intervention and the fixation-head set across datasets and model scales without retraining; test whether peer RELEVANCE matters (unrelated vs same-topic peers) and whether the effect scales with model size.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "The peer-context effect could be a raw context-length artifact rather than peer-problem presence; the length-matched filler control and prompt-length covariate isolate this, and a null after controls (pure length effect) is itself an informative finding. Peers could interfere with target accuracy (cross-talk) or change difficulty perception; we randomize position, use unrelated peers, and verify per-target accuracy. Attention-to-own-question is a coarse mechanism proxy and may be entangled with generic difficulty attention; head localization, the regression against difficulty, and the causal attention-cap with specificity controls address this, and a failure of capping to reproduce the effect would falsify the mechanistic claim (still a valuable negative result showing batch prompting works via a different route, e.g. pattern induction). Attention masking can push activations off-distribution and degrade coherence; matched-random-head controls and sample-validity checks are included, and over-capping could cause underthinking on hard items (measured on hardest strata). Self-doubt detection is noisy; we validate with human spot-checks. Multi-problem responses require careful per-target parsing of tokens/accuracy. Effects may be model-family and scale specific, and require models with clear think/answer delimiters; truncate-and-resume is compute-heavy, so we subsample."}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'isolation_induced_overthinking', 'Title': 'Alone With the Problem: Isolation-Induced Overthinking and the Attention-Fixation Mechanism Behind It', 'Short Hypothesis': 'The exact same problem induces substantially more overthinking (reasoning tokens, self-doubt loops) when presented alone than when embedded among unrelated peer problems in the same context, and this isolation-induced overthinking is causally driven by the model over-allocating attention to its lone input question. Diluting that attention—by presenting the problem among peers or by directly capping attention-to-own-question—cuts tokens without hurting accuracy.', 'Related Work': "Two observations motivate this. (1) Batch prompting (Srivastava et al. 2025) empirically reduces reasoning tokens by ~76% at preserved accuracy when queries share a context, attributing it loosely to reduced per-query effort, pattern induction, and hedging suppression—but offers NO mechanistic account and does not isolate WHICH effect matters or WHY effort drops. (2) The First Impression Problem (2025) shows overthinking is triggered by an internal bias from the input question, mechanistically via EXCESSIVE ATTENTION to the input question, and shows removing the question mid-reasoning reduces redundant reasoning. These two are unconnected. We unify them: we hypothesize batch prompting works precisely because embedding a problem among peers DILUTES the model's attention to its own question, defusing the first-impression bias that drives overthinking. Distinct from batch-prompting work (which is behavioral and treats batching as a throughput trick), from First-Impression work (which studies a single isolated problem and never varies context composition), and from all my prior overthinking proposals (which study within-single-problem internal dynamics under a fixed, isolated prompt). Our contribution: a new phenomenon (isolation vs peer-context overthinking on matched problems), a mechanistic account (attention-fixation on own question), and a causal, training-free intervention (attention dilution / capping).", 'Abstract': "Reasoning models overthink—but almost all study of this assumes a problem is presented alone, exactly the format that may most inflate it. We identify a new phenomenon: isolation-induced overthinking. The identical problem, presented alone, provokes substantially more reasoning tokens and more self-doubt loops than when it is embedded among unrelated peer problems in the same context, even though its content, difficulty, and correct answer are unchanged. We further propose a mechanism unifying two disconnected findings—that batch prompting cheaply suppresses overthinking, and that overthinking is triggered by excessive attention to the input question: we hypothesize that peer context works by DILUTING the model's attention to its own question, defusing the first-impression bias that drives redundant reflection. Across GSM8K, MATH500, AIME, and GPQA on open reasoning models (DeepSeek-R1 distills 1.5B-14B, Qwen3), we hold the target problem fixed and vary only the number and nature of surrounding peer problems, measuring per-target reasoning tokens, self-doubt behavior, and accuracy. Mechanistically, we measure attention mass directed at the target's own question tokens as a function of peer count, test whether it predicts the overthinking reduction, localize the attention-fixation heads, and causally validate by directly capping attention-to-own-question during isolated generation—testing whether this reproduces the peer-context token savings without peers. We compare against batch prompting and length steering. Our work reveals that how a problem is packaged silently sets how hard a model overthinks it, and traces this to a manipulable attention-fixation mechanism.", 'Experiments': "1) Establish isolation-induced overthinking (behavioral): For each TARGET problem (GSM8K, MATH500, AIME, GPQA), create matched contexts that present the SAME target either (a) alone, or (b) embedded among N unrelated peer problems (N in {1,2,4,8}) drawn from a different topic/difficulty, all answered in one response, with the target's position randomized. Keep the target text and correct answer fixed. Measure, on the target only: reasoning-token count, number of self-doubt/hedging events ('wait', 'let me double-check', 'actually'; via lexical cues + a reflection-direction classifier), and accuracy. Hypothesis: reasoning tokens on the target decrease monotonically with peer count at preserved accuracy. Report within-target effect sizes and dose-response over N. Include an 'irrelevant filler text' control (padding of matched length that is NOT peer problems) to test whether the effect requires PEER PROBLEMS specifically, not mere context length.\n2) Dissociation from length/position confounds: Fit a mixed-effects model of target reasoning length with random effect for target identity (difficulty) and fixed effects for peer count and raw context token-length (covariate). Test the peer-count effect after accounting for target identity and length; use the length-matched filler control to isolate peer-problem presence from prompt length. Vary target position (first vs middle vs last) to rule out pure serial-position effects.\n3) Wasted vs genuine compute: Use truncate-and-resume to estimate minimal-solve length; test whether peer context reduces WASTED tokens (beyond minimal solve) and self-doubt loops rather than genuine reasoning. Confirm accuracy is preserved or improved (as batch-prompting work reports).\n4) Attention-fixation mechanism (Location): During generation of the target's reasoning, measure the attention mass directed at the TARGET's own question tokens, as a function of peer count. Hypothesis: attention-to-own-question is highest in isolation and dilutes as peers are added, and its magnitude predicts the overthinking level (regress target reasoning tokens on attention-to-own-question vs peer count and difficulty; compare R^2). Localize the specific heads/layers whose attention-to-own-question best tracks overthinking (head attribution), connecting to the First-Impression excessive-attention mechanism.\n5) Causal validation (Causal Intervention): During ISOLATED generation (no peers), directly cap/downweight attention to the target's own question tokens on the identified fixation heads (attention masking / rescaling), and test whether this reproduces the peer-context token savings and self-doubt reduction WITHOUT any peers. Controls: cap attention on matched-random heads (no effect), cap attention to non-question tokens (specificity), off-target accuracy preserved. Report sign, dose-response (cap strength), and specificity. Reverse test: artificially amplify attention-to-own-question in a peer context and check overthinking returns.\n6) Comparison and application: Compare the attention-cap intervention and peer-context (batch) prompting against no intervention, length/verbosity steering (Efficiency Steering), and fixed truncation. Metrics: accuracy (overall and hardest strata), mean tokens, tokens-at-equal-accuracy, self-doubt-event reduction, wall-clock. Also quantify the practical implication: how much do isolated single-problem benchmark protocols OVER-estimate a model's overthinking relative to realistic multi-problem/batched deployment.\n7) Generalization: transfer the attention-cap intervention and the fixation-head set across datasets and model scales without retraining; test whether peer RELEVANCE matters (unrelated vs same-topic peers) and whether the effect scales with model size.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': 'The peer-context effect could be a raw context-length artifact rather than peer-problem presence; the length-matched filler control and prompt-length covariate isolate this, and a null after controls (pure length effect) is itself an informative finding. Peers could interfere with target accuracy (cross-talk) or change difficulty perception; we randomize position, use unrelated peers, and verify per-target accuracy. Attention-to-own-question is a coarse mechanism proxy and may be entangled with generic difficulty attention; head localization, the regression against difficulty, and the causal attention-cap with specificity controls address this, and a failure of capping to reproduce the effect would falsify the mechanistic claim (still a valuable negative result showing batch prompting works via a different route, e.g. pattern induction). Attention masking can push activations off-distribution and degrade coherence; matched-random-head controls and sample-validity checks are included, and over-capping could cause underthinking on hard items (measured on hardest strata). Self-doubt detection is noisy; we validate with human spot-checks. Multi-problem responses require careful per-target parsing of tokens/accuracy. Effects may be model-family and scale specific, and require models with clear think/answer delimiters; truncate-and-resume is compute-heavy, so we subsample.'}

