Action: FinalizeIdea
Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "diagnostic_order_anchoring", "Title": "First Symptom, Wrong Diagnosis: Premature Hypothesis Commitment as the Mechanism of Symptom-Order Anchoring in Clinical Reasoning Models", "Short Hypothesis": "For a fixed set of clinical findings pointing to a single correct diagnosis, reasoning LLMs reach different final diagnoses depending only on the ORDER in which symptoms are presented, anchoring on the diagnosis best supported by the earliest symptoms. This order effect is mechanistically caused by a decodable 'leading-hypothesis' representation that forms after the first few symptoms and resists updating when later, more specific symptoms arrive; steering/resetting this representation removes the order-induced misdiagnoses.", "Related Work": "Human clinical-reasoning research robustly documents symptom-order anchoring: early symptoms bias the initial hypothesis and later evidence is distorted toward it (Order effects in diagnostic reasoning, 2012; Effect of presentation order in clinical decision making, 1997; Evidence for Anchoring Bias During Physician Decision-Making, 2023; Information Distortion, 2013). LLM anchoring work shows numeric/framing anchors shift output distributions (Anchors in the Machine, 2025; CBEval, 2024; Anchoring bias experimental study, 2025) and locates positional/MCQ 'A'-anchoring circuits (Anchored Answers, 2024), while The First Impression Problem (2025) shows an internal preliminary guess triggers overthinking via excessive attention to the question. Crucially, NONE (a) studies symptom-PRESENTATION-ORDER anchoring in the multi-step DIFFERENTIAL DIAGNOSIS of dedicated reasoning models, (b) shows order alone flips the FINAL diagnosis on content-identical cases in a safety-critical domain, or (c) ties this to a decodable leading-hypothesis representation that forms early and fails to update. Prior LLM anchoring uses artificial numeric anchors and single-turn outputs; prior clinical-order work is behavioral in humans. We transfer the human clinical phenomenon into reasoning LLMs and give it a mechanistic (Location + Causal Intervention) account, distinct from all my prior overthinking-efficiency proposals.", "Abstract": "Physicians who see a patient's symptoms in a different order can reach a different diagnosis, a robust human anchoring effect where early findings dominate. We ask whether reasoning language models inherit this, and mechanistically why, in the high-stakes setting of differential diagnosis. We construct clinical cases whose full symptom set unambiguously supports one correct diagnosis, then present the identical findings in different orders: an order whose leading symptoms favor a plausible-but-wrong competitor diagnosis versus an order whose leading symptoms favor the correct one, plus randomized orders. Across curated diagnostic benchmarks (e.g., MedQA-derived vignettes, NEJM-style case reports, and templated multi-symptom cases with controlled hypothesis structure) on open reasoning models (DeepSeek-R1 distills 1.5B-14B, Qwen3, and a medical-tuned model), we measure whether the final diagnosis and accuracy shift with symptom order alone, using a mixed-effects decomposition to isolate order from case content. Mechanistically, we test that after the first few symptoms the residual stream linearly encodes a leading-hypothesis representation predicting the eventual diagnosis, that this representation is set early and updates too little when later contradicting symptoms arrive, and that its early strength predicts order-induced misdiagnosis. We localize it via probing and causally validate via activation patching (patch the correct-order leading-hypothesis state into a wrong-order case and observe the diagnosis correct) and steering. Finally we test a training-free 'hypothesis-reset' intervention applied after symptom intake that cancels the order effect. Because symptom order is arbitrary in real clinical notes, this reveals an order-driven misdiagnosis risk with a decodable, steerable internal cause.", "Experiments": "1) Establish symptom-order anchoring (behavioral): Build cases with a fixed symptom set S that unambiguously supports one CORRECT diagnosis but where a subset of early-presentable symptoms is also consistent with a plausible COMPETITOR diagnosis. Sources: templated multi-symptom vignettes with controlled hypothesis structure (strongest claims), plus MedQA-derived and NEJM-style case reports adapted so the full findings are diagnostic. For each case create content-identical orderings: (a) competitor-leading (early symptoms favor the wrong diagnosis, specific disambiguating symptoms last), (b) correct-leading, (c) several randomized orders. Elicit differential + final diagnosis on DeepSeek-R1 distills (1.5B/7B/8B/14B), Qwen3, and a medical-tuned model. Metrics: final-diagnosis accuracy and competitor-misdiagnosis rate per ordering. Hypothesis: competitor-leading order lowers accuracy and raises competitor-misdiagnosis vs correct-leading, on identical findings. Report within-case effect sizes. 2) Dissociation from content: Mixed-effects model of accuracy/misdiagnosis with random effect for case identity (intrinsic difficulty) and fixed effect for ordering; test significance after accounting for case identity. Control for position/length with matched-length filler and randomized disambiguating-symptom position. 3) Leading-hypothesis probing (Location): After the first k symptoms (before the full set is presented), extract residual-stream activations and train a linear probe to decode the model's current leading diagnosis. Metrics: AUROC/accuracy for predicting the eventual final diagnosis from the early state; layer sweep. Test whether early leading-hypothesis strongly predicts the FINAL diagnosis even when it is wrong (early commitment). Test separability from a generic difficulty/uncertainty probe. 4) Under-updating test: Track the probe-decoded leading hypothesis across symptoms; measure how much it shifts when a late disambiguating symptom (favoring the correct diagnosis) arrives, in competitor-leading vs correct-leading orders. Hypothesis: in competitor-leading order the representation under-updates (stays anchored), and the magnitude of under-update predicts order-induced misdiagnosis (regression R^2 vs difficulty/length baselines). 5) Causal validation (Causal Intervention): Localize the leading-hypothesis representation via activation patching; patch the correct-leading leading-hypothesis state into the matched competitor-leading case (same findings) and test whether the final diagnosis corrects. Controls: matched-random-direction patch; off-target case accuracy preserved; report sign, dose-response, specificity. Reverse patch to induce misdiagnosis. 6) Hypothesis-reset intervention (training-free): After all symptoms are presented but before the model commits, steer the leading-hypothesis representation toward a neutral/uncommitted state (subtract the early-anchor component) so the model re-weighs the full evidence. Evaluate on all datasets. Metrics: reduction in order-induced accuracy gap, competitor-misdiagnosis reduction, accuracy retention. Baselines: no intervention, prompt-based reset ('reconsider all findings without regard to order'), presenting all symptoms simultaneously (oracle order-free upper bound), and re-ordering to correct-leading (oracle). 7) Generalization: transfer probe/steering across datasets and model scales without retraining; test whether more hypotheses (>2 competitors) worsen anchoring (mirroring human findings) and whether medical-tuned models anchor more or less.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "Constructing cases where the FULL symptom set is unambiguously diagnostic yet early subsets favor a competitor is nontrivial and requires clinical validity; we anchor strongest claims on templated controlled-hypothesis cases and use adapted MedQA/NEJM vignettes with expert/LLM-judge verification, treating ambiguous cases separately. The order effect could be a position/length artifact rather than symptom-content-order per se; matched-length filler, randomized disambiguating-symptom position, and the mixed-effects analysis isolate this, and a null after controls is an informative negative result. The leading-hypothesis representation may be inseparable from a generic difficulty/uncertainty signal; the separability analysis, under-update trajectory (not just magnitude), and causal patching address this. Steering/patching can push activations off-distribution and harm accuracy; matched-random-direction controls and validity checks are included, and the reset must not overwrite genuinely correct early commitments (specificity check on correct-leading cases). The effect may be small in strong or medical-tuned models trained to weigh all findings (a valuable boundary condition). Clinical vignettes are synthetic/adapted demonstrations of the failure mode, not clinical validation of a deployable tool. Diagnosis extraction requires reliable parsing/matching; we use LLM-judge with human spot-checks. Results may be model-family and scale specific."}}
