en a late disambiguating symptom (favoring the correct diagnosis) arrives, in competitor-leading vs correct-leading orders. Hypothesis: in competitor-leading order the representation under-updates (stays anchored), and the magnitude of under-update predicts order-induced misdiagnosis (regression R^2 vs difficulty/length baselines). 5) Causal validation (Causal Intervention): Localize the leading-hypothesis representation via activation patching; patch the correct-leading leading-hypothesis state into the matched competitor-leading case (same findings) and test whether the final diagnosis corrects. Controls: matched-random-direction patch; off-target case accuracy preserved; report sign, dose-response, specificity. Reverse patch to induce misdiagnosis. 6) Hypothesis-reset intervention (training-free): After all symptoms are presented but before the model commits, steer the leading-hypothesis representation toward a neutral/uncommitted state (subtract the early-anchor component) so the model re-weighs the full evidence. Evaluate on all datasets. Metrics: reduction in order-induced accuracy gap, competitor-misdiagnosis reduction, accuracy retention. Baselines: no intervention, prompt-based reset ('reconsider all findings without regard to order'), presenting all symptoms simultaneously (oracle order-free upper bound), and re-ordering to correct-leading (oracle). 7) Generalization: transfer probe/steering across datasets and model scales without retraining; test whether more hypotheses (>2 competitors) worsen anchoring (mirroring human findings) and whether medical-tuned models anchor more or less.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': 'Constructing cases where the FULL symptom set is unambiguously diagnostic yet early subsets favor a competitor is nontrivial and requires clinical validity; we anchor strongest claims on templated controlled-hypothesis cases and use adapted MedQA/NEJM vignettes with expert/LLM-judge verification, treating ambiguous cases separately. The orde